      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Adjacent members of a homologous series differ by one CH2 unit, which has mass 12 + 2 = 14 u."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "HCOOH and CH3COOH have the same functional group (–COOH) and differ by exactly one CH2, so they are homologues. The other pairs either have different functional groups or do not differ by CH2."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "DoU = (2C + 2 + N − H)/2 = (10 + 2 + 1 − 7)/2 = 3."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true because both alcohols have the same functional group. R is true because boiling point rises with molecular mass. However, a trend in boiling point does not explain why chemical properties are similar."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t02_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Cyclohexane C6H12 fits CnH2n, not CnH2n−2, so Statement I is false. Alcohol solubility in water falls as the hydrophobic chain lengthens, so Statement II is true."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t03_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "In but-2-ene each double-bond carbon carries two different groups (H and CH3), so cis and trans forms exist. Options 2, 3 and 4 each have one double-bond carbon bearing two identical groups, so none of them can show cis-trans isomerism."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t03_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "C4H10O has 4 alcohols: butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol. It has 3 ethers: methyl propyl ether, methyl isopropyl ether and diethyl ether. Total = 7."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t03_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "C2 and C3 are both chiral and their substituents differ, so the molecule is unsymmetrical. With no meso form, the number of stereoisomers is 2^2 = 4."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t03_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A: butane and 2-methylpropane differ in carbon chain, so chain isomerism (III). B: the Cl changes position, so position isomerism (II). C: aldehyde versus ketone, so functional isomerism (IV). D: different alkyl groups on either side of the ether O, so metamerism (I)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t03_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is false: one carbon of CH2=CHCl carries two H atoms, so no geometrical isomerism. B is true: the cis isomer generally has the higher dipole moment. C is true: a racemic mixture is optically inactive. D is true: C5H12 has n-pentane, isopentane and neopentane. E is false: enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions. So B, C and D are correct."
    },
