      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "CH3+ has three bonding pairs and no lone pair, so carbon has only 6 valence electrons. It is sp2 hybridised and trigonal planar, with an empty p orbital."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t06_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Alkyl groups donate electrons by the +I effect and destabilise a carbanion. Stability therefore falls with more substitution: methyl > 1° > 2° > 3°."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t06_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The cationic carbon has three alpha carbons: two CH3 (6 H) and one CH2 (2 H). That gives 6 + 2 = 8 alpha hydrogens."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t06_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "blind_working": "A is true because the three methyl groups stabilise the cation by hyperconjugation and the +I effect. R is a true structural fact but does not explain the stability order. Both true, R not the explanation (intended answer)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t06_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Statement I is correct: the allylic cation is stabilised by pi conjugation and by hyperconjugation from the methyl C-H bonds. Statement II is incorrect: allylic and benzylic carbanions become sp2 and planar so the lone pair can delocalise."
    },
