      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A temporary, complete transfer of π electrons that occurs only while an attacking reagent is present, and reverts when it is removed, is the electromeric effect."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t08_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "blind_working": "The α-H atoms are 3 + 3 from the two CH3 groups on C+ and 2 from the CH2, giving 8 α-H and so 8 hyperconjugative structures. Some sources add the original structure to get 9; I took 8 as the intended count."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t08_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The standard -I order is NO2 > CN > COOH > F > Cl > Br > I > OH > OCH3, which matches option 1."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t08_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The tetrasubstituted alkene is more stable. Its 12 α-H give greater hyperconjugation than the 3 α-H of propene, so R correctly explains A."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u14_t08_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "The Baker-Nathan order is CH3 > C2H5 > iPr > tBu, and tBu has no α-H, so Statement I is correct. Kekulé structures are not real separate species, and the hybrid is lower in energy, not higher, so Statement II is false."
    },
